\cf2 \cb1 Here\'92s the clean way to think about it:\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A\'a0
\f1\b GPU
\f0\b0 \'a0and a\'a0
\f1\b TPU
\f0\b0 \'a0are both special chips used to speed up deep learning. A normal computer processor can do this work too, but it is usually much slower for neural networks. Google Colab offers both GPU and TPU runtimes, but simply selecting one does not guarantee your code is actually using it.\'a0\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f5\b\fs40 \cf2 GPU vs TPU\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f7\fs36 \cf2 GPU\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 A\'a0
\f1\b GPU
\f0\b0 \'a0was originally built for graphics, but it also became very good at the kind of math neural networks use - especially doing many similar calculations at the same time. TensorFlow says Keras models can run on a single GPU with no code changes in many cases, which is one reason GPUs are often the easiest acceleration option for beginners.\'a0\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f7\b\fs36 \cf2 TPU\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 A\'a0
\f1\b TPU
\f0\b0 \'a0is a chip Google designed specifically for machine learning. Google describes TPUs as custom AI accelerators optimized for training and running AI models, and notes that they are especially strong at the large grid-like number operations used in machine learning.\'a0\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f5\b\fs40 \cf2 So is a GPU \'93better\'94 for training neural networks?\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf2 Often yes in practice for students and common notebook workflows - but not always.
\f0\b0 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Why people often tell you \'93use a GPU\'94:\
\pard\intbl\itap1\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls120\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 It is usually easier to get started with in Colab and TensorFlow/PyTorch.\
\ls120\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A lot of tutorials and example code assume GPU first.\
\ls120\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Many models run on GPU with little or no code change.\'a0\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Why TPU is not automatically better for every case:\
\pard\intbl\itap1\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls121\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 TPUs shine most when the model, software, and data-loading setup fit them well.\
\ls121\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 TPUs may need more framework-specific setup than a single GPU.\
\ls121\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Some smaller or more ordinary projects do not benefit enough to make the extra setup worth it. This is an inference from Google and TensorFlow\'92s docs emphasizing TPU-specific setup and strategy APIs.\'a0\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\cf2 So the honest version is:\
\pard\intbl\itap1\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls122\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 CPU
\f0\b0 \'a0- slowest for neural network training\
\ls122\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 GPU
\f0\b0 \'a0- usually the best default choice in Colab for general deep learning work\
\ls122\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 TPU
\f0\b0 \'a0- can be extremely strong for the right workloads, but is more specialized\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f5\b\fs40 \cf2 Why training is so much faster on GPU/TPU\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 Training a neural network means repeating huge numbers of multiplications and additions again and again. TensorFlow notes that newer NVIDIA GPUs can do certain 16-bit calculations faster than standard 32-bit calculations, and TPUs can do a related faster number format called\'a0
\f1\b bfloat16
\f0\b0 \'a0faster than standard 32-bit numbers. That is part of why both can be much faster than a CPU.\'a0\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Plain-English translation:\
\pard\intbl\itap1\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls123\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A\'a0
\f1\b 32-bit number
\f0\b0 \'a0is a standard computer number format\
\ls123\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A\'a0
\f1\b 16-bit number
\f0\b0 \'a0uses less space and can often be processed faster\
\pard\intbl\itap1\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls123\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Mixed precision
\f0\b0 \'a0means using a mix of fast smaller numbers and standard numbers so training stays accurate but speeds up.\'a0\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f5\b\fs40 \cf2 What about the T4 GPU in Google Colab?\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 The\'a0
\f1\b NVIDIA T4
\f0\b0 \'a0is a specific GPU model often seen in Colab. NVIDIA lists it with\'a0
\f1\b 16 GB memory
\f0\b0 ,\'a0
\f1\b 2,560 CUDA cores
\f0\b0 , and up to\'a0
\f1\b 65 FP16 TFLOPS
\f0\b0 \'a0of mixed-precision performance. You do not need to memorize those terms yet - the main point is that a T4 is a real deep learning accelerator, not just a display chip.\'a0\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Plain-English translation of the spec terms:\
\pard\intbl\itap1\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls124\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Memory
\f0\b0 \'a0- how much data the GPU can hold while working\
\ls124\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cores
\f0\b0 \'a0- many small workers inside the chip\
\ls124\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Performance
\f0\b0 \'a0- roughly how much math it can do per second\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f5\b\fs40 \cf2 Simple analogy\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 Think of training as moving a mountain of bricks.\
\pard\intbl\itap1\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls125\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 CPU
\f0\b0 : one strong worker carrying bricks one by one\
\ls125\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 GPU
\f0\b0 : a huge crew carrying many bricks at once\
\ls125\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 TPU
\f0\b0 : a highly specialized brick-moving machine built for one kind of job\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0
\cf2 That is why training on CPU can take much longer.\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f4\b\fs48 \cf2 Advanced neural network architectures\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 These are different ways of arranging the layers and connections so the network is better suited to a particular kind of problem.\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f5\b\fs40 \cf2 1) Fully connected network\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 This is the basic kind you have been learning first.\
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 How it works:\
